course: world
lesson: 187
title: One counterexample is enough
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Big words like "all", "every", "never" and "nobody" make big claims, and big claims are fragile: one clear example the other way, a counterexample, knocks them down.
explain: Freddie says, "All swans are white." His friend shows a photo of a black swan from Australia. One black swan, and "all" is gone: "It's a swan, bro, and it's black. 'All' just died."
explain: The trick to watch for is the excuse: "That one doesn't count", "Well, that dog's weird." Calling the counterexample names doesn't make it go away.
explain: It's lopsided in a funny way: ONE example breaks "nobody can", but to PROVE "nobody can" you would have to check everybody.
explain: The fix is easy and honest: swap "all" for "most". "Most dogs can't climb trees" is true; "dogs can't climb trees" is not.
fact: One clear example that breaks an "all", "never" or "nobody" claim shows the claim is false.
fact: Black swans live in Australia, so "all swans are white" is false.
fact: Saying a counterexample "doesn't count" or is "weird" doesn't make it go away; grown-ups call this the "no true Scotsman" move.
fact: England's women's football team, the Lionesses, won the European Championship in 2022 and again in 2025; Sam Kerr and Alexia Putellas are among the world's best players.
fact: Mudskippers are fish that spend lots of time out of the water, on mud.
fact: One person doing a backflip breaks "nobody can"; to prove "nobody can" you would have to check everyone.
fact: The platypus and the echidnas (spiny anteaters of Australia and New Guinea) are mammals that lay eggs; most mammals give birth to live young.
fact: Lots of older people use technology well; a rule about everyone of one age is a stereotype.
fact: Most dogs can't climb trees well, but some can scramble up.
fact: Spiders are arachnids, not insects: spiders have 8 legs and 2 body parts, insects have 6 legs and 3 body parts.
fact: People can learn and enjoy sports at any age.
fact: In Britain it rains in every month of the year, summer included.
fact: Mercury is a metal that is liquid at room temperature, and sodium is a metal soft enough to cut with a knife.
fact: Words people use: "W" means a win; "cheat code" means a secret way round a game's rules; "that's a vibe" means "that's cool"; "dinnae" (Scotland) means don't; "hen" (Glasgow) means dear; "chuckin' it doon" (Scotland) means pouring with rain.
exercise: Freddie: "All swans are white." His friend shows a photo of a black swan. What does one black swan show? | "All swans are white" is false | Black swans aren't real swans | Most swans in the world are black | Nothing; one swan isn't enough
exercise: Where do black swans live in the wild? | Australia | Antarctica | The Arctic | The Sahara
exercise: Freddie: "That one doesn't count." What is he doing? | Brushing off a counterexample | Running a fair and careful test | Running an "if" backwards | Making a slippery slope
exercise: Sophie: "Girls can't be good at football." Which fact breaks her claim? | The Lionesses won the European Championship in 2022 and 2025 | Girls have never played in a big final | Women's football only started last year | Only boys are allowed to take penalties
exercise: Leo: "Every fish lives in water. Always." What breaks his "always"? | A mudskipper hopping on mud | A goldfish in a bowl | A shark in the sea | A trout in a river
exercise: Ava: "Nobody in our school can do a backflip." Ollie does one. What is needed to break a "nobody can"? | One person who can | Every pupil in the school | A teacher's permission | Ten backflips in a row
exercise: What would you need to PROVE "nobody in our school can do a backflip"? | To check everyone in the school | To ask one good friend who knows | To watch one video of a backflip | To try just once yourself and fail
exercise: Kai's dad: "Mammals don't lay eggs." Kai: "What about the platypus?" Which mammals lay eggs? | The platypus and the echidnas | Cats and dogs | Whales and dolphins in the sea | Bats and mice
exercise: Ruby: "Every old person hates technology." Her grandma runs a knitting channel on YouTube with 50,000 subscribers. What is Ruby's rule? | A stereotype about one age group | A fair rule tested on everyone | A true fact about older people | A rule with no exceptions at all
exercise: Oscar: "Dogs can't climb trees." Shown a video of a dog in a tree: "Well, that dog's weird." What is the true rule? | Most dogs can't climb well, but some can | No dog anywhere in the world can ever climb | All dogs climb trees easily | Only weird animals climb trees
exercise: Mia: "Spiders are insects." Her friend counts the legs: 8. How many legs do insects have? | 6 | 8 | 4 | 10
exercise: Which group do spiders belong to? | Arachnids | Insects | Crustaceans | Reptiles
exercise: Finn: "You can't skate if you're over 50." His mum, who is 52, skates past. What is true? | People can learn sports at any age | Skating stops at exactly 50 years old | Mums are not able to skate | Only children can skate
exercise: Lily: "It never rains in summer." It is raining on the first of August. In Britain it rains... | in every month of the year | only in the cold winter months | only in spring and autumn | never in August
exercise: Josh: "All metals are hard." Which metal is liquid at room temperature? | Mercury | Iron | Copper | Gold
exercise: Which is the honest way to fix an over-big claim? | "Most dogs can't climb trees" | "No dog can ever climb anything" | "All dogs climb trees" | "Weird dogs don't count"
test: The friend: "It's a swan, bro, and it's black. 'All' just died." What is the fair thing for Freddie to say? | "OK, not all swans are white." | "That swan doesn't count, bro." | "Black swans are painted." | "One swan proves nothing."
test: Sophie's brother: "Lionesses won the Euros twice in a row, Sophie. Who's 'can't' now?" What is his point? | One example knocks down "can't", and here there are loads | Only England's women can play football | Boys can't play football | The Euros don't count
test: Leo's sister: "It's literally hopping about on mud, Leo. Fish said 'nah'." What is the better rule? | "Most fish stay in water" | "No fish ever leaves water" | "Fish only live on mud" | "Mudskippers aren't fish"
test: Ollie lands his backflip. Ava: "OK, fine, nobody except you." What is Ava doing? | Admitting her claim was broken | Proving her claim was right all along | Changing the rules so she can win | Attacking Ollie for showing off
test: Kai's dad: "The platypus? That thing's a cheat code. Fine, you win." Is "mammals don't lay eggs" still true? | No; the platypus breaks it | Yes; platypuses aren't mammals | Yes; one animal doesn't count | No; all mammals lay eggs
test: Ruby's grandma: "Hates technology, does she? I've got more subscribers than your favourite gamer, darling." What has she broken? | The stereotype that every old person hates technology | The idea that knitting is popular | Her own internet connection | The rule that gamers have subscribers
test: Oscar's friend: "Weird or not, it's a dog and it's up a tree. Your rule fell out of the tree." What did Oscar do wrong? | He brushed off the counterexample as "weird" | He counted the dog's legs wrongly in the video | He said most dogs can't climb | He refused to watch the video
test: Mia's friend counts a spider's legs: eight. Why does that settle it? | Insects always have 6 legs, so a spider isn't one | Spiders lose legs as they grow | Some insects have 8 legs | Legs can't tell you anything
test: Finn's mum, 52, skates past: "Over 50, can't skate, he said. Bye, Finn!" Finn: "OK, that's a vibe, Mum." What changed his mind? | One clear counterexample | A long argument | A list of rules | Nothing; he's still sure
test: A Scottish gran: "Never rains in summer? Dinnae be daft, hen, it's August and it's chuckin' it doon." What is true? | One rainy summer day breaks "never" | It never rains in August, whatever she says | It only rains in Scotland | Summer rain doesn't count
test: The teacher: "Mercury: a metal, and runny. Don't touch it, it's poisonous." Which claim does mercury break? | "All metals are hard" | "All metals are shiny" | "Metals are found in rocks" | "Metals carry electricity"
test: In a cartoon a scientist sees one purple cow and shouts, "So ALL cows are purple!" What is the mistake? | One example can break "all", but it can't prove "all" | One example always proves "all" | Cows can't be purple in cartoons | Scientists never look at cows
exam: Identify the minimum number of counterexamples required to demonstrate that a claim concerning all members of a group is false. | One | Two | Ten | All members of the group
exam: It is given that black swans are found in Australia. Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion that all swans are white. | The assertion is false | The assertion remains true | The assertion is true of Australian swans only | No conclusion may be drawn
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dismissal of a counterexample on the ground that it is "weird"? | It leaves the counterexample in force and the claim refuted | It restores the original claim to truth | It constitutes a second counterexample | It proves the original claim conclusively
exam: Ascertain which of the following animals constitutes a mammal that lays eggs. | The platypus | The dolphin | The bat | The kangaroo
